# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc sin, cos, sinh and cosh of a square matrix send every matrix to analytic_apply (vecteur.cc), which diagonalises it over its complex eigenvalues, applies the function and transforms back. For a matrix of floats the eigenvectors' round-off stays in the result as imaginary noise, and above Digits 14 the entries come back as doubles: sin(evalf([[0,1],[-1,0]])) = [[2.99760216649e-15*i,1.17520119364],[-1.17520119364,2.99760216649e-15*i]] at Digits 12 and [[-4.3140830754274e-32-2.8865798640254e-15*i,1.1752011936438-7.1960319007501e-17*i],...] at Digits 40 (right [[0,sinh 1],[-sinh 1,0]], real: M^2 = -I, so sin M = M*sinh 1; mpmath sinm), cos(evalf([[0,1],[-1,0]])) = [[1.54308063482,-3.88578058619e-15*i],...] (right cosh 1 times the identity), cos(evalf([[1,2],[3,4]])) at Digits 40 = 14 digits. Now a square matrix of numbers with at least one float entry (integers, fractions and pi beside it) takes numeric_matrix_trig: the matrix (in radians) is divided by 2^s as in numeric_matrix_exp (patch 0063, whose scan, product and rounding it reuses), the series of the even part (cos, cosh) and of the odd part (sin, sinh) run at the entries' precision plus s+16 bits until a bound of the rest is below the last bit, and the double-angle rules S'=2*S*C, C'=C^2-S^2 (cos) or C^2+S^2 (cosh) run s times; the result is rounded back to the entries' precision. A real matrix stays real; a complex one works the same; sinh is not (e^M-e^-M)/2, which cancels for a small entry. The zero matrix gives the exact identity or zero matrix; exact and symbolic matrices keep analytic_apply.
